{{Short description|20th-century literary form and movement}}
{{Literature}}
'''Postmodern literature''' is a [[genre]] of [[fiction]] characterized by: the use of [[metafiction]], [[unreliable narrator|unreliable narration]], [[self-reference]], and [[intertextuality]] (literature building upon or reimagining other literature); tones of paranoia or [[ironic]] or [[black comedy]]; and [[theme (narrative)|themes]] of historical or current political significance. As a style of [[experimental literature]], it emerged strongly in the [[United States]] in the 1960s through the writings of [[author]]s such as [[Kurt Vonnegut]], [[Thomas Pynchon]], [[William Gaddis]], [[Philip K. Dick]], [[Kathy Acker]], and [[John Barth]]. Beginning within or alongside the [[counterculture of the 1960s]], postmodernists often challenge [[Authority|authorities]] and the [[status quo]].<ref name=":2">[[Linda Hutcheon]] (1988) ''A Poetics of Postmodernism.'' London: Routledge, pp. 202-203.</ref> This inspiration is, among other things, seen through how postmodern literature is highly self-reflexive about the political issues it speaks to.

Precursors to postmodern literature include [[Miguel de Cervantes]]' ''[[Don Quixote]]'' (1605–1615), [[Laurence Sterne]]'s ''[[Tristram Shandy]]'' (1760–1767), [[James Hogg]]'s ''[[The Private Memoirs and Confessions of a Justified Sinner|Private Memoirs and Confessions of a Justified Sinner]]'' (1824),<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Redekop |first=Magdalene |date=1985 |title=Beyond Closure: Buried Alive with Hogg's Justified Sinner |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2872832 |journal=ELH |volume=52 |issue=1 |pages=159–184 |doi=10.2307/2872832 |jstor=2872832 |issn=0013-8304|url-access=subscription }}</ref> [[Thomas Carlyle]]'s ''[[Sartor Resartus]]'' (1833–1834),<ref>{{Cite web |last=Campbell |first=Ian |date=2012-04-10 |title=Retroview: Our Hero? |url=https://www.the-american-interest.com/2012/04/10/retroview-our-hero/ |access-date= |website=[[The American Interest]]}}</ref> and [[Jack Kerouac]]'s ''[[On the Road]]'' (1957),<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Johnson|first=Ronna C.|date=2000|title="You're Putting Me on": Jack Kerouac and the Postmodern Emergence|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25112494|journal=College Literature|volume=27|issue=1|pages=22–38|jstor=25112494 |issn=0093-3139}}</ref> but postmodern literature was particularly prominent in the 1960s and 1970s. In the 21st century, [[American literature]] still features a strong current of postmodern writing, like the [[post-irony|postironic]] [[Dave Eggers]]' ''[[A Heartbreaking Work of Staggering Genius]]'' (2000)<ref>{{Cite news|date=2019-03-22|title=Looking Back at 'A Heartbreaking Work of Staggering Genius' (Published 2019)|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/03/22/books/review/dave-eggers-heartbreaking-work-of-staggering-genius.html|access-date=2020-10-20|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> and [[Jennifer Egan]]'s ''[[A Visit from the Goon Squad]]'' (2010).<ref>{{Cite thesis|last=Leal|first=Carissa M.|date=2017-08-10|title=The Progression of Postmodern Irony: Jennifer Egan, David Foster Wallace and the Rise of Post-Postmodern Authenticity|url=https://dash.harvard.edu/handle/1/37736743|language=en}}</ref> These works also further develop the postmodern form.<ref>Tore Rye Andersen (2001) "[http://ojs.statsbiblioteket.dk/index.php/passage/article/view/1995/1778 Ned med oprøret! - David Foster Wallace og det postironiske] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818123352/http://ojs.statsbiblioteket.dk/index.php/passage/article/view/1995/1778|date=2016-08-18}}" in ''Passage'', 37, 13-25.[http://ojs.statsbiblioteket.dk/index.php/passage/article/view/1995/1778] </ref>

Sometimes the term "postmodernism" is used to discuss many different things ranging from [[architecture]] to historical theory to [[philosophy]] and [[film]]. Because of this fact, several people distinguish between several forms of postmodernism and thus suggest that there are three forms of postmodernism: (1) Postmodernity is understood as a [[historical period]] from the mid-1960s to the present, which is different from the (2) theoretical postmodernism, which encompasses the theories developed by thinkers such as [[Roland Barthes]], [[Jacques Derrida]], [[Michel Foucault]] and others, and (3) "cultural postmodernism", which includes film, literature, visual arts, etc. that feature postmodern elements. Postmodern literature is, in this sense, part of cultural postmodernism.<ref>Paula Geyh (2003) "Assembling Postmodernism: Experience, Meaning, and the Space In-Between". ''College Literature'' 30:2, 1-29.</ref>

{{short description|Nationalist movement in the German physics community in the early 1930s}}
{{Italic title}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=August 2015}}

[[File:Philippe Lenard - Deutsche Physik - 2. Band - Akustik und Wärmelehre, München Lehmann, 1936-1937.jpg |thumb |200 px| [[Philipp Lenard]]. Deutsche Physik, 2. Band – Akustik und Wärmelehre. München, 1936–1937]]

'''''Deutsche Physik''''' ({{IPA|de|ˈdɔʏtʃə fyˈziːk|lang}}, {{lit.}} "German Physics") or '''Aryan Physics''' ({{langx|de|Arische Physik}})  was a [[nationalist]] movement in the [[Germany|German]] [[physics]] community in the early 1930s which had the support of many eminent physicists in Germany. The term appears in the title of a four-volume physics textbook by Nobel laureate [[Philipp Lenard]] in the 1930s.

''Deutsche Physik'' opposed the work of [[Albert Einstein]], who was ethnically Jewish, and other modern theoretically-based physics, which was disparagingly labeled '''Jewish Physics''' ({{langx|de|<b>Jüdische Physik</b>}}).

